#### I sell LifeWave X39 patches. Before you buy anything from me, you should know exactly what they are, what the evidence does and doesn't show, and why I decided to try them anyway.

> *Disclosure: I am an Independent LifeWave Brand Partner. If you buy through [my LifeWave store](https://lifewave.com/4229387), I earn a commission. Nothing here is medical advice. LifeWave products have not been evaluated by the FDA and are not intended to diagnose, treat, cure, or prevent any disease.*

Regular readers know how I work. I don't take a press release at face value, whether it comes from Congress, a drug company, or a wellness company. So when I decided to start selling a health product, I held it to the same standard I'd hold anyone else to. Here's what I found.

## What X39 is

LifeWave is a San Diego company that sells wearable patches through direct selling, meaning independent partners like me sell them instead of stores. Its best-known product is the X39 patch, invented by LifeWave's CEO, David Schmidt.

The X39 is a small adhesive patch you wear on the skin. According to LifeWave, it doesn't put anything into your body. The company calls it "phototherapy": it says the patch reflects infrared light your body emits, which raises your blood levels of a copper peptide called GHK-Cu.

GHK-Cu itself is real and well studied. It's a small, naturally occurring peptide found in blood, saliva, and urine, and levels drop as we age. In lab and animal studies, it's been linked to wound healing and the production of collagen and elastin, and creams containing it have shown skin benefits in cosmetic studies.

X39 isn't a drug, and it isn't held to the drug approval process. It puts nothing into your body, and products like it are sold as general wellness products, a low-risk category the FDA has said it does not intend to review, as long as they're marketed for things like energy, sleep, and well-being rather than to treat disease. That's why every LifeWave product carries the "not evaluated by the FDA" line.

## Where the science is encouraging

Phototherapy isn't fringe science. Under its scientific name, photobiomodulation, it's an established research field with a large body of peer-reviewed work indexed by the National Institutes of Health.

- **It calms inflammation.** A 2017 review by photomedicine researcher Michael Hamblin found that red and near-infrared light lowers inflammation in many lab and clinical settings, calling it "one of the most reproducible effects" of the therapy.
- **Researchers understand how it works.** A 2016 review by de Freitas and Hamblin traced the process to mitochondria, the energy centers of our cells. Light absorbed there triggers signals linked to repair and recovery.
- **The field is still growing.** A 2025 review in *Lasers in Medical Science* found phototherapy promising for regulating the immune system, while noting that standard dosing and long-term safety data are still needed.

GHK-Cu, the copper peptide at the heart of X39, has its own track record. A 2015 review led by Loren Pickart, the scientist who discovered GHK, reported that blood levels fall from about 200 ng/mL at age 20 to 80 ng/mL by age 60, and that the peptide speeds wound healing in skin. A 2018 review added evidence of tissue repair in the lungs, bone, liver, and stomach lining.

One honest note: the NIH-indexed phototherapy studies used lasers and LED lights, not patches. LifeWave says X39 works by reflecting the body's own light rather than using a powered device. So this research shows the science behind phototherapy and GHK-Cu is real, but it doesn't test X39 itself. That's why LifeWave's own studies, and independent ones down the road, matter.

## What LifeWave's studies found

LifeWave has paid for its own X39 studies. Some readers will stop right there, but that's how much of medicine works: drug companies pay for the clinical trials the FDA reviews before approving their drugs. X39 doesn't even face that requirement, since it isn't a drug. Lighter oversight cuts both ways, though. No FDA approval is needed, but no FDA reviewer has checked the results either. That's why the studies below, and the larger one on the way, matter.

**The GHK-Cu study (2021).** In a double-blind study of 60 adults aged 40 to 80, published in *Internal Medicine Research Open Journal*, GHK-Cu levels rose over the week in the people wearing the real patch and fell in the placebo group, a statistically significant difference. A second study reported short-term improvements in sleep and memory over seven days.

**The 2025 clinical study.** In March 2025, NIS Labs, a contract research lab in Klamath Falls, Oregon, completed a clinical study of X39 for LifeWave. Twelve healthy adults aged 44 to 73 wore the X39 patch on one clinic visit and a placebo patch on another, without knowing which was which. Then they wore X39 daily for four weeks. The researchers reported:

- **Signs of new mitochondria.** The cells' mitochondria, their energy centers, grew rapidly and remained elevated, which the researchers interpreted as evidence that the cells were building new mitochondria. In lab stress tests, the participants' blood cells also held up better against oxidative and inflammatory stress.
- **Lower inflammation markers.** C-reactive protein (CRP), a standard blood test for inflammation, dropped significantly within two hours compared with placebo and remained below baseline at four weeks. Anti-inflammatory signals increased, and several pro-inflammatory signals decreased.
- **More energy, self-reported.** Participants reported more energy and sharper focus from day one, though in a group this small, those changes weren't statistically significant.
- **No adverse events.**

**A larger trial is coming.** In July 2026, LifeWave registered a 100-person randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial of X39 and GHK-Cu blood levels on ClinicalTrials.gov, the government's public trial registry. It's scheduled to run at KGK Science in Ontario, Canada, starting in January 2027.

Here's what the studies so far can't tell us yet:

- **They're small and short.** The largest had 60 people. The 2025 study had 12, and its four-week phase had no placebo group for comparison.
- **They haven't been independently replicated** by researchers with no ties to the company.
- **They haven't reached the major journals yet.** Earlier studies appeared in lower-profile journals, and the 2025 study hasn't been published in a peer-reviewed journal. Its authors list publishing as a next step.
- **Better blood markers aren't the same as a health result.** Changes in GHK-Cu, CRP, or mitochondria are promising signs, but they don't prove the patch improves anyone's health.

## What critics say

I'd be doing you a disservice if I left this part out.

In 2023, Paul Knoepfler, a stem cell researcher at UC Davis, reviewed the X39 evidence and wasn't convinced. He pointed to small study sizes, no registration of the trials on the government's ClinicalTrials.gov database, and journals he doesn't consider rigorous. He called the proposed chain, from light to GHK-Cu to stem cell activity, "dubious." Since then, LifeWave has started registering its trials on ClinicalTrials.gov, including the 100-person trial described above.

In July 2025, Jonathan Jarry of McGill University's Office for Science and Society went further. He reviewed LifeWave's research going back to 2005 and noted that two studies on athletic performance found no benefit. His conclusion was that any benefits people feel are likely placebo.

Skeptics play a useful role. I'd rather you hear them from me than discover them later.

## What regulators have done

LifeWave has also enforced its marketing rules when sellers crossed the line. The Direct Selling Self-Regulatory Council, an industry watchdog run by BBB National Programs, has stepped in twice:

- **March 2025:** it flagged social media posts by LifeWave sellers calling X39 "scientifically proven" to boost energy, reduce pain, and improve sleep, as well as posts promising high incomes. LifeWave didn't try to defend the claims. It had the posts taken down and suspended one repeat offender.
- **April 2026:** it flagged seller posts suggesting the patches could help with serious conditions, including Alzheimer's, as well as additional income claims. Again, LifeWave had them removed.

To be fair to the company, it acted both times. But the lesson for anyone buying is simple: if a seller tells you X39 treats a disease, they're making a claim the company itself won't stand behind. That includes me. You won't hear it from me.

## Why I'm trying it anyway

Some of you have followed my health story on My Ledger. For years I dealt with a string of problems that were treated one at a time, until a rare adrenal condition finally tied them together. That journey taught me two things: take an active role in your own health, and stay skeptical of anyone selling certainty.

I'm not wearing X39 because I think it fixes my condition. It doesn't, and I'm under a doctor's care for that. I'm wearing it as part of a general wellness routine, the same way someone might try a new supplement, a sleep habit, or an exercise plan, and see how they feel.

## My results so far

I came into this with my usual skepticism and, frankly, some disdain for anything sold through multi-level marketing. So I tracked what I could measure.

- **Sleep.** Before X39, I was getting 3.5 to 4 hours of sleep a night, and that had been my routine for years. I hadn't needed an alarm clock to wake up for work since 2014. My demeanor was awful. I was a pain to be around, and I managed to agitate everyone I worked with. But after just two weeks on the patch, I began sleeping at least 6.5 to 7 hours a night.
- **Kidney function.** For the past couple of years, my kidney numbers have been falling. My eGFR, short for estimated glomerular filtration rate, is a calculation from a routine blood test that estimates how well your kidneys filter waste from your blood. The National Kidney Foundation considers 90 or higher normal, while 60 to 89 may signal early-stage kidney disease. Mine was 96 in January 2025, then fell to 73 by December 2025 and 66 by August 2026. My most recent test, taken in October 2026 after I started X39, came back at 100. My Cushing's syndrome is still untreated, and in my situation, I would not expect numbers like these to turn around on their own.

![](egfr-trend-chart.png)

- **Tinnitus.** A little over two years ago, in the middle of an ordinary day, a piercing, constant tone started in my left ear, and it never stopped. I asked my endocrinologist whether any of my diabetes medications, including the tirzepatide I take, could be causing it. An ear, nose, and throat specialist found no physical reason for it, and I was told it could be an early sign of dementia. So I learned to live with it. After two weeks on X39, the tone was noticeably quieter. On day 28, I added LifeWave's Glutathione patch, and it dropped even further. When I took that patch off for about four hours, the noise crept back up slightly, though it stayed lower than before I started. Some will call that coincidence or placebo. Maybe, but one day isn't enough to judge either way, so I'm continuing the experiment.

I do not believe this is a placebo effect. I didn't expect it to work, and I wasn't looking for reasons to believe it did. Still, this is one person, a few weeks, and one blood test, and lab numbers like eGFR can move for other reasons too. That's why I'll keep tracking and keep reporting, good or bad. Over the long term, my results will speak for themselves.

I'm not the only one, either. Many customers have shared testimonials saying X39 turned out to be more than a gimmick.

*This is what happened to me, not a promise of what will happen to you, and it isn't medical advice. If you have kidney concerns, tinnitus, or any other medical condition, talk to your doctor.*

> ***For the record, I didn’t talk to my doctors before starting. I see a lot of them, and in my experience, most look down on anything outside conventional medicine, with a few exceptions like vitamin D, B12, or iron. That doesn’t change my advice to you: if you have a medical condition or take medications, tell your doctor what you’re trying, even if you expect an eye roll.***

## How I'll sell it

Here's my promise, in the same spirit as everything else I write:

- **I'll never tell you X39 treats, cures, or prevents anything.** The evidence doesn't support it, and the law doesn't allow it.
- **I'll tell you what it costs.** At retail, it's well over $100 a month.
- **I'll tell you to talk to your doctor first,** especially if you have a medical condition, take medications, or have blood pressure concerns. Some users have reported side effects online, like skin irritation, headaches, and vivid dreams. Those are anecdotes, not clinical findings, but your doctor should know what you're trying to do.

If you want to try it after reading all of this, you can order through [my LifeWave store](https://lifewave.com/4229387), and I'm glad to answer questions before you do. If you decide it's not for you, that's a perfectly good decision too.

